      "claim": "In primates, 4-phenylbutyric acid had very low brain penetration (<0.006% uptake), providing a distant negative analog that argues against transferable CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "contradictory"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The closest and multiple structurally related analogs demonstrate direct in-vivo brain uptake and BBB penetration after systemic administration, including high brain-to-blood ratios and lack of P-gp efflux. Distant negative analogs do not override this consistent positive pattern.",
